{
  "dom_id": "step-34545",
  "name": "Example: \n                    The Mean of an Exponential Distribution",
  "problem": "Given that $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\\right),$ what is $\\textrm{E}[X]?$",
  "worked_solution": "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Exp}({\\lambda})$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X \\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\\right),$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] =\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\\right)}=\\sqrt 3\n$$",
